<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romsdal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romsdal is a traditional district of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> around the Romsdalsfjord, in the county of Møre og Romsdal, with Molde and Åndalsnes as its main towns. It is known for steep mountains, including the Trollveggen rock wall. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> it formed part of the domain of the earls of Møre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romsdal.jpg" alt="Romsdal" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pilum at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romsdal-region.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Romsdal lies between Nordmøre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunnmore">Sunnmøre</a> and traditionally takes in the areas of Aukra, Molde, Rauma and Vestnes municipalities with the old Sandøy and Fræna municipalities. It is named after the valley of Romsdalen, through which the river Rauma flows to the Romsdalfjord; the Old Norse name Raumsdalr probably means the dale of Rauma. The valley is ringed by the Romsdalsalpene range, whose Romsdalhorn, 1,550 metres high, has been compared to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matterhorn">Matterhorn</a>, and Trollveggen, the north face of Trollryggen, is the tallest vertical cliff in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Legend makes the district the inheritance of Raum the Old, son of Nór, whom Hversu Noregr byggðist names as the founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and the Laxdæla saga names it as the home of Ketill Flatnose, a chieftain of the 850s who conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and the Isle of Man. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>, Romsdal was a petty kingdom. Ragnvald Eysteinsson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarl">jarl</a> of Møre and Harald's supporter, probably lived on or near Veøya, the district's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> hub of trade; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkneyinga-saga">Orkneyinga saga</a> claims his son Hrolf Ganger was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> of Normandy, which historians dispute.</p><p>King Eystein I died at Hustad in 1122. Late in the 12th century Veøy Church was built over an ancient pagan site. In the 1600s the Romsdal market opened at Devold as an outlet for the Lesja ironworks; it moved to Veblungsnes in 1820, and Molde later took over its role. A Scottish mercenary force that landed at Isfjorden on its way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> was stopped at the Battle of Kringen. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> of 1658 ceded the land north of the Romsdalfjord to Sweden, local farmers defied Swedish taxes and conscription until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-copenhagen-1660">Treaty of Copenhagen</a> restored the region in 1660.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The largest town, Molde, is the seat of Møre og Romsdal County Municipality. Åndalsnes, near the mouth of the Rauma, is the terminus of the Rauma Line railway from Dombås; British troops landed there in April 1940, after the German invasion, as part of a move to retake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>. The hairpin road of Trollstigen leads south from Åndalsnes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Romsdal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Møre, the coastal land between <b>Romsdal</b> and the fjords to the south, was a petty kingdom in the 8th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-more">Earldom of Møre</a></li></ul></section>
